   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the arts and cultural affairs law, in relation to renaming\nagency building one, agency building two, agency building three and agency\nbuilding four, located at the empire state plaza",
    "basePrintNo": "S8722",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8722-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4.07, Arts & Cul L",
    "lawSection": "Arts and Cultural Affairs Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8722\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the arts and cultural affairs law, in relation to renam-\ning agency building one, agency building two, agency building three and\nagency building four, located at the empire state plaza\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo rename agency buildings one through four at the Empire state plaza.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new subdivision 12 to section 4.07 of the arts and\ncultural affairs law to provide a process for renaming agency building\none through four on the Empire State plaza.\n\nSection 2 is the after enactment.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Governor Nelson A. Rockefeller Empire State Plaza is one of the most\nspectacular  capital centers in the country. It contains major buildings\nlike the Egg Performance Center, Cultural Education Center  as  well  as\noffice space for thousands of state employees\n\nMany  of  the  Plaza  buildings  are named after famous people including\nErastus Corning, the longest tenured mayor of any large city the  United\nStates  (Corning  Tower)  and Robert Abrams , a long time State Attorney\nGeneral, (The Robert Abrams Building for Law and Justice ).    The  four\nbuilding that house thousands of state employees are named 1,2,3, and 4.\n\nThe  naming  of  building  simple  numbers misses a great opportunity to\neducate New Yorkers. This legislation honors New York's most significant\nnatural resource, its famous  rivers.  The  founding  of  the  state  is\ndirectly  linked  to  the  Hudson  River and other rivers have served as\neconomic drivers of the country.\n\nThis bill sets up a process for the selection of the names by  using  an\nexisting Commission. The Commission will recommend river designations to\nthe  Governor.  She will be the ultimate decision maker. New Yorkers who\nvisit will be reminded of the great  natural  resources  of  the  Empire\nState.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nOne hundred and eighty days after enactment\n",
